course: dictionary
lesson: 11177
title: One word, many meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: One word can have several meanings, and the sentence around it tells you which one to use. «The winning team scores the most points.» Here winning describes the team, so it is an adjective meaning the one that wins.
explain: «We are winning!» shows winning as a verb, the -ing form of win, and it is happening right now. Ask yourself: is the word describing a thing, showing an action, or naming money? The clues in the sentence will tell you.
explain: «She gave a winning smile.» A smile is not a game, so winning here means pleasant and charming. Check each meaning by putting it into the sentence and seeing if it still makes sense.
fact: Winning can be an adjective, a verb, or part of a noun-like word: winnings.
fact: «The winning goal» means the goal that wins the match.
fact: «A winning smile» means a pleasant and charming smile.
fact: «Winnings» means money won in a game or competition.
fact: In a debate there is no score, so «winning» just means who seemed better.
exercise: «In the final minute, the winning goal flew into the net.» What does winning mean here? | Losing badly | Pleasant and charming | Money won in a game | *Being the one that wins
exercise: «Her winning smile made everyone in the room feel welcome.» What does winning mean here? | Being the one that wins | Money won in a game | Scoring no points at all | *Pleasant and charming
exercise: «Max's winnings from the quiz were spent on new books.» What does winnings mean? | Points lost in a match | A pleasant smile | The team that wins the most matches | *Money won in a game or competition
test: «We are winning!» What form of the word win is winning here? | The plural noun form | The past tense of win | *The -ing form of win | A pleasant, charming form
test: In a debate with no score, what does «winning» mean? | *Who seemed better | The person who spoke the longest and loudest | The team with the highest score | The side that ran out of time
exam: Which phrase uses winning as an adjective, describing a noun? | we are winning | keep winning | his winnings | *a winning goal
